# 2. COMPACT AND RESPONSIVE USER INTERFACE

The application currently needs a more compact desktop experience.

## 2.1 Mini Window

Create a small, resizable main window optimized for everyday use.

Requirements:

- Use a compact layout rather than a large dashboard.
- Display the most frequently used capture actions immediately.
- Keep unnecessary descriptions, oversized cards, and empty spaces to a minimum.
- Use compact icons with clear tooltips.
- Keep text readable at common Windows scaling settings.
- Support resizing without broken layouts.
- Remember the last window size and position when appropriate.
- Prevent the window from reopening off-screen.
- Respect Windows display scaling and multiple monitors.

Recommended initial layout:

- A compact title bar
- Capture buttons
- Recording button
- Recent captures
- A small toolbar or navigation area
- Settings accessible without cluttering the main window

Treat these dimensions as initial design targets, not rigid requirements. Choose the smallest practical default window that keeps the controls understandable.

## 2.2 Expanded Workspace

Some tasks need more room.

Provide an expanded workspace for:

- Image editing
- Annotation
- OCR results
- Video trimming
- Capture history
- Settings

The mini window must remain compact when these features are not being used.

## 2.3 Visual Design

Use a consistent, professional Windows-friendly design.

Include:

- Light and dark themes
- Consistent spacing and typography
- Clear hover, focus, disabled, and active states
- Keyboard accessibility
- Helpful tooltips
- Empty states
- Loading states
- Clear error messages
- Confirmation for destructive actions
- No unnecessary animations
- No fake progress indicators

Do not make the interface visually complicated merely to make it look feature-rich.

