# 3. SYSTEM TRAY INTEGRATION

Implement a real Windows notification-area system-tray icon using the chosen desktop framework.

A webpage icon or simulated tray button is not sufficient.

## 3.1 Tray Menu

Provide these actions:

- Open PixelGrip
- Capture Full Screen
- Capture Selected Region
- Capture Active Window
- Start Screen Recording
- Stop Screen Recording when recording is active
- Open Recent Captures
- Open Settings
- Check for Updates, if update support is implemented
- Quit PixelGrip

Only display actions that are supported and currently available.

## 3.2 Close and Minimize Behaviour

Add configurable settings:

- Minimize to tray when minimized, if supported by the framework
- Close button hides the application to the tray
- Ask for confirmation before exiting during an active recording
- Quit completely when selected from the tray menu

Requirements:

- Hiding the window must not terminate the application.
- Tray actions must restore the correct window.
- The application must not create duplicate tray icons.
- Recording must not stop unexpectedly when the main window is hidden.
- Show a visible indication when recording is active.
- Provide an accessible way to quit if the main window is hidden.

Default behaviour: clicking the window's Close button hides PixelGrip to the system tray. The user can change this behaviour in Settings.

Do not make the application impossible to exit.

## 3.3 Tray Reliability

Test:

- Repeated opening and hiding
- Multiple monitor setups
- Display resolution changes
- Windows Explorer restarting
- Application shutdown
- Active recording
- Tray icon cleanup

Use the desktop framework's actual tray APIs.

---

# 4. STARTUP AND BACKGROUND BEHAVIOUR

Provide optional Windows startup integration.

Requirements:

- A setting to start PixelGrip when the user signs in.
- Startup disabled by default unless existing user preferences require otherwise.
- Respect user consent and Windows policies.
- Do not use unnecessary background services.
- Do not continuously capture screenshots or record audio/video in the background.
- Do not keep high-CPU background loops running.
- Do not request administrator privileges unless a documented feature genuinely requires them.

The app should remain idle and lightweight when no task is active.

