# 13. LIGHTWEIGHT BUILD AND SMALL INSTALLER

Reducing application size is a major priority.

## 13.1 Dependency Audit

Review all dependencies.

- Remove unused packages.
- Remove duplicate libraries.
- Avoid large libraries for small tasks.
- Avoid bundling several libraries that perform the same function.
- Use tree-shaking where supported.
- Lazy-load large feature modules.
- Split code into logical chunks.
- Optimize icons, fonts, and images.
- Avoid loading recording or OCR libraries on the main screen.
- Keep development dependencies out of production runtime bundles.

Do not remove a dependency until all usages have been verified.

## 13.2 Desktop Framework

Evaluate Tauri 2 and Electron against the actual requirements.

Compare:

- Installer size
- Installed size
- RAM usage
- Startup time
- System-tray reliability
- Global hotkeys
- Multi-monitor capture
- Recording and audio support
- Native window support
- Build complexity
- Security
- Long-term maintenance

Do not switch frameworks solely because one is generally advertised as smaller.

If using Tauri, implement required native functionality through appropriate plugins or Rust commands.

If using Electron, keep the main process minimal and isolate renderer code from privileged operations.

Do not build both frameworks unless there is a clear reason.

## 13.3 Lazy Loading

Load advanced modules only when requested.

Examples:

- OCR module loads when OCR is opened.
- Video-processing module loads when video processing is requested.
- PDF export module loads when PDF export is selected.
- Editor-specific functionality loads when opening the editor.

Lazy loading must not make the first use unreliable or leave buttons permanently loading.

## 13.4 Memory and CPU

- Release temporary image buffers.
- Dispose of unused media streams.
- Stop recording tracks when recording ends.
- Clean up event listeners.
- Close unnecessary native windows.
- Avoid repeated full-size image copies.
- Avoid unnecessary polling.
- Keep idle CPU usage low.
- Handle large images without crashing.
- Avoid memory leaks during repeated capture and editing.

## 13.5 Measure Results

After optimization, report:

- Original installer size
- New installer size
- Percentage reduction
- Installed size
- Cold-start time
- Idle RAM usage
- CPU usage while idle
- Build and test results

If a feature increases size substantially, explain why and consider optional modules or downloadable components.

Never promise a specific installer size before measuring a real release build.

